      "author_association": "NONE",
      "body": "While going through the socket-chunking cases here, I noticed another spot that assumes a `recv()`-boundary-independent marker count, in a method not covered by #36729: the response-drain loop at the end of `check_slow_read_throttle` in `test/functional/interface_http.py`:\n\n```python\nnum_res = 0\nwhile num_res < count + 2:\n    response = conn.conn.sock.recv(10 * 1024 * 1024)\n    num_res += response.count(b\"HTTP/1.1 200\")\n```\n\n`response.count(b\"HTTP/1.1 200\")` is evaluated on each individual `recv()` chunk. If a `recv()` boundary splits the `HTTP/1.1 200` marker across two chunks (e.g. one ends `...HTTP/1.`, the next starts `1 200...`), it is counted in neither, so `num_res` undercounts and the loop can't reach `count + 2`, blocking until the socket timeout. A ~10 MB drain is quite likely to span multiple `recv()` calls, so this looks like the same latent class.\n\nCounting over the accumulated buffer avoids the split-marker case:\n\n```python\nbuf = bytearray()\nnum_res = 0\nwhile num_res < count + 2:\n    data = conn.conn.sock.recv(10 * 1024 * 1024)\n    if not data:\n        break\n    buf += data\n    num_res = buf.count(b\"HTTP/1.1 200\")\n```\n\nFound by inspection; I haven't run it under the forced-chunking reproducer. Happy to open a PR if it's useful and not already covered elsewhere.\n",
